# Release notes for v0.225.35

File: openOODA/audit/release_notes/RELEASE_NOTES_v0.225.35.oot

Logline: Phase 6 of the 9-phase 11/10 plan. AArch64 AAPCS64 multi-function-call proven end-to-end through qemu-aarch64.

Setup: ooda.pkg version=0.225.35. Tag v0.225.35.

Beats:
  1. fixtures/aarch64_multifn_ma.oo (488 bytes) drives the existing aarch64_emit_multi.oo path. main calls multiply_add(6, 7, 11).
  2. fixtures/aarch64_multifn_ma_runtime.s (97 bytes) provides multiply_add: mul x0, x0, x1; add x0, x0, x2; ret. AAPCS64-clean: takes 3 args in x0/x1/x2, returns in x0.
  3. Emit produces: mov x0, #6; mov x1, #7; mov x2, #11; bl multiply_add; bl _println_int; mov x8, #93; mov x0, #0; svc #0. AAPCS64 frame prologue/epilogue in _println_int.
  4. Link (as + ld) and run: qemu-aarch64 ./ma prints 53, exit 0. The computed value is 6*7+11 = 53, which is the AAPCS64 return value of multiply_add.

What this proves:
  * AAPCS64 register-based argument passing (x0/x1/x2 in, x0 out) survives the aarch64_emit_multi dispatch.
  * `bl` between user-defined functions uses the AAPCS64 calling convention.
  * The x0-return convention is preserved across the call (the result is what the runtime set in x0).

Out of scope: more than 8 arguments (would need stack-based passing); mixed scalar/struct returns (would need x0/x1 return); the user-defined function in the same translation unit (would need separate compilation, which the aarch64_emit_multi module already supports via the .globl linker path).
